  {slug:"battle-of-vedrosha",name:"Battle of Vedrosha",type:"event",dates:"1500",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-vedrosha.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Vedrosha was fought on 14 July 1500 on the Vedrosha River near Dorogobuzh in western Russia. The Muscovite army of Ivan III defeated the Lithuanian army of Grand Duke Alexander, and its commander Konstanty Ostrogski was captured. The defeat allowed Muscovy to keep the Chernigov and…"},
  {slug:"dutch-republic",name:"Dutch Republic",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/dutch-republic.jpg",desc:"The Dutch Republic was the federal state of the United Provinces of the Netherlands that emerged from the revolt against Spain in the late sixteenth century. A leading commercial and naval power in the seventeenth century, it was a model and rival for England, and its financial and commercial…"},
  {slug:"mexico-city",name:"Mexico City",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/mexico-city.jpg",desc:"Mexico City is the capital of Mexico, built on the ruins of the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan. After the Spanish conquest, Cortes deliberately razed the Aztec city and founded the new colonial capital on its site. It became the seat of the viceroyalty of New Spain."},
  {slug:"bago",name:"Bago",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bago.jpg",desc:"Bago, formerly Pegu, is a city in southern Myanmar, north-east of Yangon. It was the capital of the Mon kingdom of Hanthawaddy from 1369, of the Toungoo kings Tabinshwehti and Bayinnaung in the 16th century, when it was one of the great cities of South-East Asia, and of the Restored Hanthawaddy…"},
  {slug:"senegambia",name:"Senegambia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/senegambia.jpg",desc:"Senegambia is the region of West Africa between the Senegal and Gambia rivers. It was dominated for a time by the Jolof Empire and was later home to the Kaabu Empire. It became an early zone of Atlantic trade, including the slave trade, with European merchants."},
  {slug:"thomas-aquinas",name:"Thomas Aquinas",type:"person",dates:"1225–1274",img:"/images/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas.jpg",desc:"Thomas Aquinas was a thirteenth-century Italian Dominican friar and theologian, the most influential philosopher of medieval scholasticism. His synthesis of Christian theology and Aristotelian philosophy drew on Latin translations of Aristotle and of commentaries by Muslim thinkers. The…"},
  {slug:"tomato",name:"Tomato",type:"term",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/tomato.jpg",desc:"The tomato is a fruit-bearing plant native to the Americas that spread to Europe and the rest of the world after the Spanish conquests of the sixteenth century. It became a staple of many cuisines. Accounts of the Spanish Empire list it, alongside maize, potato, cassava and sweet potato, among…"},
  {slug:"luanda",name:"Luanda",type:"place",dates:"1576",img:"/images/encyclopedia/luanda.jpg",desc:"Luanda is the capital and largest city of Angola, on the Atlantic coast. It was founded in 1575–76 by the Portuguese captain Paulo Dias de Novais as São Paulo de Loanda and became the centre of Portuguese Angola and one of the busiest slave-trading ports in the Atlantic world. The Dutch held it…"},
  {slug:"amasya",name:"Amasya",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/amasya.jpg",desc:"Amasya is a city in northern Anatolia, Turkey, in the narrow gorge of the Yeşilırmak river. As ancient Amaseia it was a capital of the kings of Pontus, whose rock tombs line the cliffs, and the birthplace of the geographer Strabo. Later held by the Danishmendids and Seljuks, under the Ottomans it…"},
  {slug:"ardennes",name:"Ardennes",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ardennes.jpg",desc:"The Ardennes is a region of wooded hills and plateaus spread across south-eastern Belgium, Luxembourg and north-eastern France, with a fringe in Germany. Thinly settled and long a frontier between French and German lands, it held medieval abbeys such as Stavelot and Malmedy. In the Second World…"},
  {slug:"rogier-van-der-weyden",name:"Rogier van der Weyden",type:"person",dates:"c. 1399–1464",img:"/images/encyclopedia/rogier-van-der-weyden.jpg",desc:"Rogier van der Weyden was an Early Netherlandish painter, official painter of the city of Brussels from 1436. His emotionally intense works, such as the Descent from the Cross now in the Prado and the Last Judgement altarpiece for the Hospices de Beaune, were widely copied across Europe. He also…"},
  {slug:"river-medway",name:"River Medway",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/river-medway.jpg",desc:"The River Medway flows through Kent in south-eastern England to the Thames estuary. In 43 CE the Roman invasion army fought a two-day battle at a crossing of the river, probably near Rochester. Its estuary later held the naval dockyard at Chatham, and in June 1667 a Dutch fleet under Michiel de…"},
  {slug:"ferdinand-i-of-aragon",name:"Ferdinand I of Aragon",type:"person",dates:"1380–1416",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon.jpg",desc:"Ferdinand I, known as Ferdinand of Antequera, was King of Aragon from 1412 to 1416. A Castilian prince of the Trastámara dynasty and regent of Castile, he won fame by taking Antequera from Granada in 1410. Chosen by the Compromise of Caspe, he crushed the revolt of James II of Urgell and helped…"},
  {slug:"mahendravarman-i",name:"Mahendravarman I",type:"person",dates:"reigned c. 600–630",img:"/images/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i.jpg",desc:"Mahendravarman I was king of the Pallava dynasty of Kanchipuram in the early 7th century. He fought the Chalukya king Pulakeshin II, excavated the first rock-cut temples of the Tamil country and wrote the Sanskrit farce Mattavilasa Prahasana. According to tradition he was converted from Jainism…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-white-mountain",name:"Battle of White Mountain",type:"event",dates:"1620",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain.jpg",desc:"The Battle of White Mountain was fought near Prague on 8 November 1620, early in the Thirty Years' War. Imperial and Catholic League troops defeated the army of the Bohemian Estates and their elected king, Frederick V of the Palatinate. The defeat led to the re-Catholicisation of Bohemia, the…"},
  {slug:"hirohito",name:"Hirohito",type:"person",dates:"1901–1989",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hirohito.jpg",desc:"Hirohito was Emperor of Japan from 1926 until his death in 1989. His reign saw Japanese militarism, expansion in Asia and the Pacific War. He announced Japan's surrender in 1945, and under the post-war constitution he remained emperor as a symbolic head of state, ending the Empire of Japan as a…"},
  {slug:"plantation-of-ulster",name:"Plantation of Ulster",type:"event",dates:"1609",img:"/images/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster.jpg",desc:"The Plantation of Ulster was the organised colonisation of Ulster by English and Scottish Protestant settlers from 1609. It followed the Nine Years' War and the Flight of the Earls in 1607, after which the lands of the O'Neills, O'Donnells and other Gaelic lords were confiscated by the crown. It…"},
  {slug:"kaunas",name:"Kaunas",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kaunas.jpg",desc:"Kaunas, historically Kovno, is the second-largest city of Lithuania, at the confluence of the Neris and Nemunas rivers. Grown around a 14th-century castle, it was the seat of the German Ober Ost military administration during the First World War and served as Lithuania's temporary capital from…"},
  {slug:"ge-hong",name:"Ge Hong",type:"person",dates:"283–343",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ge-hong.jpg",desc:"Ge Hong was a Chinese scholar, alchemist and Daoist writer of the Eastern Jin period from the region south of the Yangtze. His Baopuzi (The Master Who Embraces Simplicity) gathered teachings on alchemy, longevity and the pursuit of immortality, alongside essays on government and society. He also…"},
  {slug:"lake-champlain",name:"Lake Champlain",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/lake-champlain.jpg",desc:"Lake Champlain is a long, narrow lake between New York and Vermont that extends north into Quebec and drains into the St Lawrence through the Richelieu River. It is named after Samuel de Champlain, who reached it in 1609 and fought the Mohawk on its shores. As the water route between the Hudson…"},
  {slug:"harihara-i",name:"Harihara I",type:"person",dates:"r. c. 1336–1356",img:"/images/encyclopedia/harihara-i.jpg",desc:"Harihara I was the founder, with his brother Bukka I, of the Vijayanagara Empire and its first ruling house, the Sangama dynasty. Having probably served the Kampili or Hoysala rulers, the brothers established their kingdom on the Tungabhadra around 1336 as Delhi Sultanate authority in the south…"},
  {slug:"dmitry-donskoy",name:"Dmitry Donskoy",type:"person",dates:"1350–1389",img:"/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy.jpg",desc:"Dmitry Donskoy was Grand Prince of Moscow from 1359 and of Vladimir from 1362. He built the first stone Kremlin in Moscow and made Moscow the leading Russian principality over Tver and Ryazan. His victory over the Golden Horde commander Mamai at the Battle of Kulikovo in 1380, on the Don, gave…"},
  {slug:"angouleme",name:"Angoulême",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/angouleme.jpg",desc:"Angoulême is a city in south-western France on a hill above the Charente, capital of the Charente department. From 866 it was the seat of counts descended from Vulgrin I, later the Taillefer line and the Lusignans, and it passed to the French crown in 1308. Its domed Romanesque cathedral of…"},
  {slug:"abbasid-caliphate",name:"Abbasid Caliphate",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate.jpg",desc:"The Abbasid Caliphate was the Islamic caliphate that replaced the Umayyads in 750 and ruled from Baghdad, which it founded as its capital. Its early centuries were a golden age of trade, scholarship and science, but its political authority later fragmented among regional dynasties such as the…"},
  {slug:"herat",name:"Herat",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/herat.jpg",desc:"Herat is a city in western Afghanistan, historically a major centre of Khorasan. Under the Timurid Empire, especially in the fifteenth century, it became a capital and a leading centre of Persian art, literature and scholarship. It was the home of the Herat school of miniature painting."},
  {slug:"saint-domingue",name:"Saint-Domingue",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/saint-domingue.jpg",desc:"Saint-Domingue was the French colony on the western part of the Caribbean island of Hispaniola. In the eighteenth century its sugar and coffee plantations, worked by enslaved Africans, made it one of the most profitable colonies in the world. The Haitian Revolution that began in 1791 ended French…"},
  {slug:"ketef-hinnom",name:"Ketef Hinnom",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ketef-hinnom.jpg",desc:"Ketef Hinnom is an archaeological site south-west of the Old City of Jerusalem, where burial caves of the late Iron Age were excavated in 1979. They yielded two tiny rolled silver amulets inscribed with a version of the Priestly Blessing from the Book of Numbers. Usually dated to the late 7th or…"},
  {slug:"hoysala-empire",name:"Hoysala Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire.jpg",desc:"The Hoysala Empire was a kingdom in southern India, centred on present-day Karnataka, known for its ornate temple architecture. Its capital was Dwarasamudra. The Delhi Sultanate's general Malik Kafur extracted great tribute from the Hoysalas during his southern campaigns in the early fourteenth…"},
  {slug:"otumba",name:"Otumba",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/otumba.jpg",desc:"Otumba is a town in the State of Mexico, north-east of Mexico City, close to the ruins of Teotihuacan. Nearby obsidian sources were worked by the Teotihuacanos and later by the Aztecs, for whom Otumba was a craft centre. On the plain near the town Hernán Cortés defeated an Aztec army in July…"},
  {slug:"second-schleswig-war",name:"Second Schleswig War",type:"event",dates:"1864",img:"/images/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war.jpg",desc:"The Second Schleswig War was fought in 1864 between Denmark and the allied German powers Prussia and Austria. It began after Denmark adopted a constitution binding Schleswig more closely to the kingdom, contrary to earlier agreements. After defeat at Dybbøl and the loss of Jutland, Denmark ceded…"},
  {slug:"ennius",name:"Ennius",type:"person",dates:"239–169 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ennius.jpg",desc:"Quintus Ennius was a Latin poet, born in 239 BCE at Rudiae in the Messapian south of Italy. Brought to Rome by Cato, he wrote tragedies, comedies and satires, and his epic Annales, telling the history of Rome in hexameters, made him known as the father of Latin poetry. He died in 169 BCE."},
  {slug:"edinburgh",name:"Edinburgh",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/edinburgh.jpg",desc:"Edinburgh is the capital of Scotland, on the southern shore of the Firth of Forth. Its castle rock was Din Eidyn, the stronghold of the British kingdom of Gododdin, until the Northumbrians took it, probably in 638. From the 15th century it was Scotland's capital, the seat of the royal court and…"},
  {slug:"artemis",name:"Artemis",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/artemis.jpg",desc:"Artemis was the Greek goddess of hunting, the wilderness, wild animals and childbirth, daughter of Zeus and Leto and twin of Apollo, born, according to myth, on the island of Delos. She was worshipped throughout the Greek world, and her temple at Ephesus was counted among the Seven Wonders. The…"},
  {slug:"new-jersey",name:"New Jersey",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/new-jersey.jpg",desc:"New Jersey is a state of the United States between the Hudson and Delaware rivers. Taken by England from the Dutch in 1664, it was granted to Lord Berkeley and Sir George Carteret and divided into East and West Jersey before being reunited as a royal province in 1702. In 1776 it adopted a state…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-bubat",name:"Battle of Bubat",type:"event",dates:"1357",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bubat.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Bubat took place in 1357 at Bubat, near the Majapahit capital in East Java. According to the Pararaton and the Kidung Sunda, King Linggabuana of Sunda came to Majapahit to marry his daughter Dyah Pitaloka to King Hayam Wuruk, but the minister Gajah Mada demanded that she be given as…"},
  {slug:"philopoemen",name:"Philopoemen",type:"person",dates:"c. 253–182 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/philopoemen.jpg",desc:"Philopoemen was a general and statesman of Megalopolis who served eight times as strategos of the Achaean League. He reformed the league's army on the Macedonian model, defeated the Spartan tyrant Machanidas at Mantinea in 207 BCE and brought Sparta into the league in 192 BCE. Captured by the…"},
  {slug:"laconia",name:"Laconia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/laconia.jpg",desc:"Laconia is a region of the south-eastern Peloponnese in Greece, centred on the Eurotas valley between the Taygetus and Parnon mountains. In the Mycenaean age it held the kingdom remembered in Homer as that of Menelaus, and from the Archaic period it was the territory of Sparta. It is now a…"},
  {slug:"caernarfon",name:"Caernarfon",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/caernarfon.jpg",desc:"Caernarfon is a royal town on the Menai Strait in Gwynedd, north-west Wales. It grew beside the Roman fort of Segontium, and after the conquest of Wales Edward I built Caernarfon Castle there from 1283 as the seat of English government in North Wales. The castle, part of a World Heritage Site,…"},
  {slug:"late-bronze-age",name:"Late Bronze Age",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age.jpg",desc:"The Late Bronze Age is the period of roughly the sixteenth to the twelfth centuries BCE in the Near East and eastern Mediterranean. It was an era of great territorial powers, including the Egyptian New Kingdom, the Hittite Empire, Mitanni, Babylon and Assyria, linked by diplomacy, trade and…"},
  {slug:"sucre",name:"Sucre",type:"place",dates:"1538",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sucre.jpg",desc:"Sucre is the constitutional capital of Bolivia, founded by the Spanish in 1538 as La Plata and also called Chuquisaca. From 1559 it was the seat of the Royal Audiencia of Charcas, governing Upper Peru and the silver mines of Potosí, and it had an archbishopric and the University of San Francisco…"},
  {slug:"crimean-war",name:"Crimean War",type:"event",dates:"1853–1856",img:"/images/encyclopedia/crimean-war.jpg",desc:"The Crimean War was fought from 1853 to 1856 between the Russian Empire and an alliance of the Ottoman Empire, Britain, France and Sardinia. Much of the fighting centred on the siege of Sevastopol in Crimea. Russia's defeat exposed its backwardness and prompted major reforms, including the…"},
  {slug:"hongwu-emperor",name:"Hongwu Emperor",type:"person",dates:"1328–1398",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor.jpg",desc:"The Hongwu Emperor, born Zhu Yuanzhang, was a peasant-born rebel leader who founded the Ming dynasty in 1368. He drove the Mongol Yuan court out of China, ending the Yuan Dynasty. As emperor he built a highly centralised and autocratic state, laying the foundations of the Ming Empire."},
  {slug:"all-india-muslim-league",name:"All-India Muslim League",type:"group",dates:"1906",img:"/images/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league.jpg",desc:"The All-India Muslim League was a political party founded at Dhaka in 1906 to represent the interests of Muslims in British India. Under Muhammad Ali Jinnah it adopted the Lahore Resolution of 1940 calling for separate Muslim states, and its success in the 1946 elections led to the creation of…"},
  {slug:"clement-attlee",name:"Clement Attlee",type:"person",dates:"1883–1967",img:"/images/encyclopedia/clement-attlee.jpg",desc:"Clement Attlee was a British Labour politician and prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1945 to 1951. Deputy prime minister in Churchill's wartime coalition, he led Labour to a landslide victory in 1945; his government nationalised major industries, created the National Health Service and…"},
  {slug:"first-mithridatic-war",name:"First Mithridatic War",type:"event",dates:"89–85 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war.jpg",desc:"The First Mithridatic War was fought from 89 to 85 BCE between Rome and Mithridates VI of Pontus. Mithridates overran Roman Asia, ordered the massacre of Romans and Italians there in 88 BCE and won over many Greek cities, including Athens. Sulla took Athens and defeated his armies at Chaeronea…"},
  {slug:"gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares",name:"Gaspar de Guzmán, Count-Duke of Olivares",type:"person",dates:"1587–1645",img:"/images/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares.jpg",desc:"Gaspar de Guzmán, Count-Duke of Olivares, was the chief minister of Philip IV of Spain from 1621 to 1643. He sought to restore Spanish power in the Thirty Years' War and to share its costs among all the king's realms through the Union of Arms. His demands provoked the revolts of Catalonia and…"},
  {slug:"portuguese-language",name:"Portuguese language",type:"term",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-language.jpg",desc:"Portuguese is a Romance language that originated in the western Iberian Peninsula. Spread by the Portuguese Empire, it is now spoken by over 250 million people in Brazil, Angola, Mozambique and other countries. It was also used in the administration of the Kongo Empire after its rulers adopted…"},
  {slug:"midwestern-united-states",name:"Midwestern United States",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states.jpg",desc:"The Midwestern United States is a region of the north-central United States, including states such as Illinois, Ohio, Michigan, Wisconsin, Minnesota, Iowa and Missouri. Before European settlement it was home to the Mississippian culture, centred on Cahokia, and later to peoples such as the…"},
  {slug:"allies",name:"Allies",type:"group",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/allies.jpg",desc:"The Allies, or Allied Powers, were the coalition that fought the Central Powers in the First World War, including France, the British Empire, Russia, Italy and later the United States. Their victory in 1918 led to the dissolution of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. The peace treaties they imposed…"},
  {slug:"cantabrian-wars",name:"Cantabrian Wars",type:"event",dates:"29–19 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars.jpg",desc:"The Cantabrian Wars were Rome's final conquest of the Iberian Peninsula, fought from 29 to 19 BCE against the Cantabri and Astures of the northern mountains. Augustus took personal command in 26–25 BCE, and his general Agrippa completed the subjugation in 19 BCE after fierce resistance and…"},
  {slug:"lower-nubia",name:"Lower Nubia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/lower-nubia.jpg",desc:"Lower Nubia is the stretch of the Nile valley between the First and Second Cataracts, in southern Egypt and northern Sudan. Home to the A-Group culture, it was repeatedly conquered by Egypt, which ruled it as Wawat under the Viceroy of Kush in the New Kingdom. In late antiquity the Blemmyes and…"},
  {slug:"nobel-peace-prize",name:"Nobel Peace Prize",type:"institution",dates:"1901",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize.jpg",desc:"The Nobel Peace Prize is one of the prizes established by the will of Alfred Nobel and awarded annually since 1901 by a committee of the Norwegian parliament. Laureates who led modern states include Óscar Arias of Costa Rica in 1987, Juan Manuel Santos of Colombia in 2016 and Abiy Ahmed of…"},
  {slug:"firth-of-forth",name:"Firth of Forth",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth.jpg",desc:"The Firth of Forth is the estuary of the River Forth on the east coast of Scotland, with Edinburgh on its southern shore and Fife to the north. In Roman and early medieval times it marked a frontier zone between the Votadini and Gododdin to the south and the Picts to the north. Northumbrian power…"},
  {slug:"sviatoslav-i-of-kiev",name:"Sviatoslav I of Kiev",type:"person",dates:"c. 942–972 CE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev.jpg",desc:"Sviatoslav I of Kiev was a prince of Kievan Rus in the tenth century. In the 960s he campaigned against the Khazars, destroying their power. Accounts of the Khazar Khaganate treat his campaigns as decisive in the khaganate's decline."},
  {slug:"islam",name:"Islam",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/islam.jpg",desc:"Islam is a monotheistic religion founded in Arabia in the seventh century CE through the teaching of the Prophet Muhammad, centred on the Quran. It shaped the founding and governance of many empires, from the Rashidun, Umayyad and Abbasid caliphates to the Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires. It…"},
  {slug:"expedition-of-the-thousand",name:"Expedition of the Thousand",type:"event",dates:"1860",img:"/images/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand.jpg",desc:"The Expedition of the Thousand was the campaign led by Giuseppe Garibaldi in 1860, in which about a thousand volunteers in red shirts sailed from near Genoa and landed at Marsala in Sicily in May. They defeated the Bourbon forces, took Palermo and crossed to the mainland, entering Naples in…"},
  {slug:"feudalism",name:"Feudalism",type:"term",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/feudalism.jpg",desc:"Feudalism is a term for systems in which land was granted in return for military service and loyalty, creating hierarchies of lords and vassals. Originally applied to medieval Europe such as the Holy Roman Empire, it is used more loosely for comparable arrangements elsewhere, including the…"},
  {slug:"narmer",name:"Narmer",type:"person",dates:"r. c. 3100 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/narmer.jpg",desc:"Narmer was an Egyptian king of around 3100 BCE, the last ruler of the Thinite line of Dynasty 0 and usually counted as the founder of the First Dynasty. The Narmer Palette, a carved ceremonial slate, shows him wearing the crowns of both Upper and Lower Egypt and is often read as a record of the…"},
  {slug:"sigmund-freud",name:"Sigmund Freud",type:"person",dates:"1856–1939",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud.jpg",desc:"Sigmund Freud was an Austrian neurologist who founded psychoanalysis, working in Vienna from the late nineteenth century. His theories of the unconscious mind had a profound influence on psychology and culture. Accounts of the Austro-Hungarian Empire name him among the figures of Vienna's…"},
  {slug:"devaraja",name:"Devaraja",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/devaraja.jpg",desc:"Devaraja ('god-king') was a religious and political concept associating the ruler with a divine power, usually Shiva. It was central to the Khmer Empire from its foundation under Jayavarman II, legitimising royal authority and the building of state temples such as those at Angkor. Comparable…"},
  {slug:"beijing",name:"Beijing",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg",desc:"Beijing is a city in northern China that has served as the capital of several imperial dynasties. As Dadu (Khanbaliq) it was the capital of the Yuan Dynasty, and the Ming Empire moved its capital there in the early fifteenth century, building the Forbidden City. Its fall to rebels and then to the…"},
  {slug:"layla-and-majnun",name:"Layla and Majnun",type:"work",dates:"1188",img:"/images/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun.jpg",desc:"Layla and Majnun is a Persian narrative poem by Nizami Ganjavi, completed in 1188 at the request of the Shirvanshah Akhsitan I. Based on an Arabic tale of the poet Qays, driven mad by his hopeless love for Layla, it became one of the best-known love stories of the Islamic world. It forms part of…"},
  {slug:"athena",name:"Athena",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/athena.jpg",desc:"Athena was the ancient Greek goddess of wisdom, crafts and war, the virgin daughter of Zeus, said to have sprung fully armed from his head. Patron of Athens, where the Parthenon was her temple, she was worshipped in sanctuaries across the Greek world, at Lindos, Smyrna and Troy among others,…"},
  {slug:"conradin",name:"Conradin",type:"person",dates:"1252–1268",img:"/images/encyclopedia/conradin.jpg",desc:"Conradin was the last legitimate member of the Hohenstaufen dynasty, Duke of Swabia and claimant to the kingdoms of Sicily and Jerusalem. At fifteen he invaded Italy to recover Sicily from Charles of Anjou, but was defeated at the Battle of Tagliacozzo in 1268. Captured while fleeing, he was…"},
  {slug:"chan-chan",name:"Chan Chan",type:"place",dates:"c. 850–1470",img:"/images/encyclopedia/chan-chan.jpg",desc:"Chan Chan was the capital of the Chimú kingdom on the north coast of Peru, near modern Trujillo, and the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian Americas. It contained some ten walled royal compounds with courts, storerooms and burial platforms decorated with relief friezes. The Incas conquered…"},
  {slug:"uruk",name:"Uruk",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/uruk.jpg",desc:"Uruk was one of the oldest and largest cities of ancient Sumer, in southern Mesopotamia, and is associated with the early development of writing and urban life. It was the city of the legendary king Gilgamesh. Uruk features in the rise of the Akkadian Empire and of the Third Dynasty of Ur, and it…"},
  {slug:"new-amsterdam",name:"New Amsterdam",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam.jpg",desc:"New Amsterdam was the Dutch colonial settlement on Manhattan Island, the chief town of the colony of New Netherland. It passed to England and was renamed New York, with the transfer confirmed in 1674. Accounts of the Dutch Empire count its loss among the setbacks that stalled Dutch trade growth…"},
  {slug:"sonderbund-war",name:"Sonderbund War",type:"event",dates:"1847",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sonderbund-war.jpg",desc:"The Sonderbund War was a brief civil war in Switzerland in November 1847, in which the federal army under General Guillaume-Henri Dufour defeated the Sonderbund, a league of seven Catholic cantons, in under a month and with roughly a hundred dead. It was the last armed conflict on Swiss soil, and…"},
  {slug:"luba-empire",name:"Luba Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/luba-empire.jpg",desc:"The Luba Empire was a Central African state in the Upemba region of the south-eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, flourishing from about the sixteenth to the late nineteenth century. It was built around a sacred kingship and a network of tributary chiefdoms, with institutions such as the…"},
  {slug:"free-imperial-city",name:"Free imperial city",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city.jpg",desc:"A free imperial city was a city in the Holy Roman Empire that was subject only to the emperor rather than a territorial prince. Such cities had self-government and representation in the Imperial Diet. Most lost their status in the Napoleonic reorganisation, though a few survived as city-states…"},
  {slug:"royal-air-force",name:"Royal Air Force",type:"institution",dates:"1918",img:"/images/encyclopedia/royal-air-force.jpg",desc:"The Royal Air Force is the air force of the United Kingdom, founded on 1 April 1918 as the world's first independent air service. Between the wars it policed parts of the Middle East from airfields across Iraq and the Gulf, including Sharjah, on the air route to India. It defended Britain in the…"},
  {slug:"domitian",name:"Domitian",type:"person",dates:"51–96 CE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/domitian.jpg",desc:"Domitian was Roman emperor from 81 to 96 CE, the last of the Flavian dynasty. He strengthened the Danube frontier, fought wars against the Dacians, the Chatti and the Sarmatian Iazyges, and carried out a large building programme in Rome. His autocratic style and the treason trials of his later…"},
  {slug:"plutarch",name:"Plutarch",type:"person",dates:"c. 46–after 119",img:"/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg",desc:"Plutarch was a Greek writer and philosopher from Chaeronea in Boeotia, who also served as a priest at Delphi. His Parallel Lives pair biographies of famous Greeks and Romans, and his Moralia gather essays on ethics, religion and customs. His works are a major source for ancient history and…"},
  {slug:"brahmins",name:"Brahmins",type:"group",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/brahmins.jpg",desc:"Brahmins are the priestly and learned class at the top of the traditional Hindu social hierarchy, responsible for religious ritual and the transmission of sacred knowledge. Under the Gupta Empire they received land grants and royal patronage. Brahmanical norms shaped Gupta law and society,…"},
  {slug:"illinois",name:"Illinois",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/illinois.jpg",desc:"Illinois is a state of the Midwestern United States, lying between Lake Michigan and the Mississippi river, with Springfield as its capital and Chicago as its largest city. Named after the Illinois Confederation, it was part of the French Illinois Country before passing to Britain in 1763 and…"},
  {slug:"ryazan",name:"Ryazan",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ryazan.jpg",desc:"Ryazan is a city in western Russia on the Oka river, south-east of Moscow. Called Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky until 1778, it replaced Old Ryazan, destroyed by the Mongols in 1237, as the seat of the princes of Ryazan in the fourteenth century. The principality was annexed by Moscow in 1521, and the…"},
  {slug:"baji-rao-i",name:"Baji Rao I",type:"person",dates:"1700–1740",img:"/images/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i.jpg",desc:"Baji Rao I was the Peshwa, or chief minister, of the Maratha state from 1720 to 1740. A brilliant cavalry commander, he led rapid campaigns that extended Maratha power deep into northern India, raiding as far as Delhi. His expansion made the Peshwa the dominant power within the Maratha Empire."},
  {slug:"manila-galleon",name:"Manila galleon",type:"institution",dates:"1565–1815",img:"/images/encyclopedia/manila-galleon.jpg",desc:"The Manila galleon was the Spanish trade route across the Pacific between Manila and Acapulco, in operation from 1565 to 1815. Galleons carried American silver to Asia and returned with Chinese silk, porcelain and other goods. The trade linked the Spanish Empire to China and was one of the first…"},
  {slug:"paropamisadae",name:"Paropamisadae",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/paropamisadae.jpg",desc:"Paropamisadae was an ancient province in the Hindu Kush region of present-day Afghanistan, around the area of modern Kabul. It was among the eastern satrapies of Alexander's empire inherited by Seleucus I. Following his conflict with Chandragupta Maurya, Seleucus ceded these eastern territories…"},
  {slug:"johannes-gutenberg",name:"Johannes Gutenberg",type:"person",dates:"c. 1400–1468",img:"/images/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg.jpg",desc:"Johannes Gutenberg was a fifteenth-century German craftsman who developed printing with movable metal type in Europe. His press transformed the spread of knowledge in Europe. In China, Bi Sheng had invented movable type around 1040 CE under the Song Dynasty, about four centuries earlier."},
  {slug:"somme-river",name:"Somme (river)",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/somme-river.jpg",desc:"The Somme is a river of northern France, flowing about 245 km west through Picardy past Saint-Quentin, Amiens and Abbeville to the English Channel. Known in antiquity as the Samara, it gave its name to the Gallic town of Samarobriva, later Amiens. Its valley was the scene of the Battle of the…"},
  {slug:"ethiopian-highlands",name:"Ethiopian highlands",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands.jpg",desc:"The Ethiopian highlands are a large mountainous plateau in the Horn of Africa. Their fertile land and natural defences made them the heartland of the Aksumite Empire and its Christian successors, and their invasion by the Adal Sultanate in the sixteenth century came close to destroying the…"},
  {slug:"ludwig-railway",name:"Ludwig Railway",type:"work",dates:"1835",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-railway.jpg",desc:"The Ludwig Railway was the first railway in Germany worked by steam locomotives, opened on 7 December 1835 between Nuremberg and Fürth, a distance of about 6 kilometres. Named after Ludwig I of Bavaria, it was built by a private company, and its first locomotive, the Adler, came from Robert…"},
  {slug:"grand-council-qing",name:"Grand Council (Qing)",type:"institution",dates:"c. 1729",img:"/images/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing.jpg",desc:"The Grand Council was a small advisory body established by the Yongzheng Emperor around 1729. It handled sensitive military and political matters and concentrated decision-making in the emperor's hands, becoming the most important organ of government in the Qing Empire. The Italian Empire link…"},
  {slug:"jose-antonio-paez",name:"José Antonio Páez",type:"person",dates:"1790–1873",img:"/images/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez.jpg",desc:"José Antonio Páez was a cattle-plains leader whose llanero cavalry fought for Venezuelan independence under Simón Bolívar, winning at Las Queseras del Medio in 1819 and sharing the victory of Carabobo in 1821. Commanding in Venezuela within Gran Colombia, he led the Cosiata revolt of 1826 and the…"},
  {slug:"justo-rufino-barrios",name:"Justo Rufino Barrios",type:"person",dates:"1835–1885",img:"/images/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios.jpg",desc:"Justo Rufino Barrios was a Guatemalan general who led the Liberal Revolution of 1871 and ruled as president from 1873 to 1885. He expropriated church lands, expelled the Jesuits and built roads, railways and schools. His promotion of coffee estates relied on forced Maya labour. He was killed in…"},
  {slug:"basil-ii",name:"Basil II",type:"person",dates:"r. 976–1025",img:"/images/encyclopedia/basil-ii.jpg",desc:"Basil II was Byzantine emperor from 976 to 1025, one of the most successful rulers of the empire. After a long war he conquered the Bulgarian state, earning the epithet 'the Bulgar-Slayer'. At his death the Byzantine Empire was at its greatest extent since the seventh century."},
  {slug:"horus",name:"Horus",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/horus.jpg",desc:"Horus was an ancient Egyptian sky god, shown as a falcon or a falcon-headed man. In myth he was the son of Osiris and Isis who avenged his father against Set, and every living king was seen as Horus on earth, using a Horus name written in a serekh. His early cult centre was Nekhen, and he was…"},
  {slug:"pan-africanism",name:"Pan-Africanism",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pan-africanism.jpg",desc:"Pan-Africanism is a movement that promotes the unity and solidarity of African peoples in Africa and the diaspora. Ethiopia's victory over Italy at Adwa in 1896 made it a powerful symbol for pan-African activists. The Ethiopian Empire became a symbolic centre of the movement and hosted the…"},
  {slug:"balkh",name:"Balkh",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/balkh.jpg",desc:"Balkh, known to the Greeks as Bactra, is an ancient city in northern Afghanistan, near Mazar-i-Sharif, once the capital of Bactria. It was a centre of Achaemenid, Greco-Bactrian, Kushan and early Islamic rule and a hub of Buddhist and Zoroastrian learning, later called the 'mother of cities'. The…"},
  {slug:"bushehr",name:"Bushehr",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bushehr.jpg",desc:"Bushehr is a port city on the Persian Gulf coast of southern Iran. In the eighteenth century, together with Bandar Abbas, it handled Persian Gulf trade under the Afsharid Empire. The trade continued after the disruption of the preceding decades but was not sufficient to fund Nader Shah's military…"},
  {slug:"inanna",name:"Inanna",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/inanna.jpg",desc:"Inanna was the Sumerian goddess of love, war and fertility, identified with the Akkadian Ishtar. Enheduanna, daughter of Sargon of Akkad and high priestess at Ur, composed hymns to her and is regarded as the first named author in history. Her cult forms part of the religious background of the…"},
  {slug:"tanintharyi-region",name:"Tanintharyi Region",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region.jpg",desc:"Tanintharyi Region, historically Tenasserim, is the southernmost region of Myanmar, a long coastal strip between the Andaman Sea and the Tenasserim Hills along the border with Thailand. Contested for centuries between Burmese and Siamese kingdoms, it was ceded to Britain after the First…"},
  {slug:"stralsund",name:"Stralsund",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/stralsund.jpg",desc:"Stralsund is a port city in Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania, Germany, on the Strelasund opposite the island of Rügen. Granted town rights in 1234 by Prince Wizlaw I of Rügen, it became one of the leading Hanseatic towns and gave its name to the Treaty of Stralsund of 1370, which marked the league's…"},
  {slug:"florence",name:"Florence",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/florence.jpg",desc:"Florence is a city in Tuscany, central Italy, and a leading centre of the Renaissance. Byzantine émigré scholars contributed greatly to the revival of Greek learning there, and the Council of Florence (1438–1445) sought to reunite the Byzantine and Latin churches in the last years of the…"},
  {slug:"eastern-orthodox-church",name:"Eastern Orthodox Church",type:"idea",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church.jpg",desc:"The Eastern Orthodox Church is the body of Christian churches that follow the Byzantine tradition and recognise the authority of the ecumenical councils, separating from Rome in the Middle Ages. It was the state church of the Byzantine Empire and later of the Russian Empire, and it remained the…"},
  {slug:"pratapgad",name:"Pratapgad",type:"place",dates:"1656",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pratapgad.jpg",desc:"Pratapgad is a hill fort in the Satara district of Maharashtra, western India, built in 1656 on the orders of Shivaji to guard the passes of the Western Ghats. Below its walls in November 1659 Shivaji killed the Bijapur general Afzal Khan, and his forces then defeated the Bijapuri army. The fort…"},
  {slug:"don-quixote",name:"Don Quixote",type:"work",dates:"1605–1615",img:"/images/encyclopedia/don-quixote.jpg",desc:"Don Quixote is a novel by Miguel de Cervantes, published in two parts in 1605 and 1615. It tells of a Spanish gentleman who, his mind turned by reading chivalric romances, sets out as a knight-errant with his squire Sancho Panza. Mixing comedy, satire and reflection on reality and fiction, it is…"},
  {slug:"saxon-wars",name:"Saxon Wars",type:"event",dates:"772–804",img:"/images/encyclopedia/saxon-wars.jpg",desc:"The Saxon Wars were the campaigns fought from 772 to 804 by Charlemagne against the Saxons, a Germanic people living east of the Rhine. The conflict involved repeated annual campaigns, mass deportations and the forced Christianisation of the Saxons. Accounts of the Carolingian Empire present the…"},
  {slug:"bremerhaven",name:"Bremerhaven",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bremerhaven.jpg",desc:"Bremerhaven is a port city at the mouth of the Weser on the North Sea, an exclave of the German state of Bremen. It was founded in 1827 by Bremen's mayor Johann Smidt on land bought from Hanover, because silting of the Weser kept large ships from reaching Bremen. It became a major emigration port…"},
  {slug:"amnisos",name:"Amnisos",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/amnisos.jpg",desc:"Amnisos is an ancient harbour site on the north coast of Crete, east of Heraklion, that served as a port of Knossos. Excavations revealed a Minoan villa with frescoes of lilies, and the nearby cave of Eileithyia, goddess of childbirth, is mentioned in Homer's Odyssey. Its name appears in the…"},
  {slug:"galilean-moons",name:"Galilean moons",type:"term",dates:"1610",img:"/images/encyclopedia/galilean-moons.jpg",desc:"The Galilean moons are the four largest moons of Jupiter, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, discovered by Galileo Galilei with his telescope in January 1610. He published the discovery in the Sidereus Nuncius and named them the Medicean Stars in honour of Cosimo II de' Medici and his brothers,…"},
  {slug:"matchlock",name:"Matchlock",type:"term",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/matchlock.jpg",desc:"The matchlock was an early firearm mechanism in which a slow-burning match was lowered onto the priming powder to fire the gun. Matchlock muskets spread across Eurasia in the sixteenth century. In the Mughal Empire, musketeers armed with matchlocks fought alongside cavalry and artillery."},
  {slug:"justin-i",name:"Justin I",type:"person",dates:"c. 450–527 CE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/justin-i.jpg",desc:"Justin I was a Byzantine emperor of the early sixth century, uncle of Justinian I. According to the site, a major Aksumite naval expedition across the Red Sea was undertaken at his request. It defeated the Himyarite king Dhu Nuwas and extended the Aksumite Empire's control over South Arabia."},
  {slug:"antigonus-ii-gonatas",name:"Antigonus II Gonatas",type:"person",dates:"c. 320–239 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas.jpg",desc:"Antigonus II Gonatas was king of Macedon and son of Demetrius Poliorcetes. His victory over the Gauls at Lysimachia in 277 BCE won him the Macedonian throne, and he established the Antigonid dynasty firmly in Macedon. He fought the Chremonidean War against Athens and Sparta and kept garrisons in…"},
  {slug:"romagna",name:"Romagna",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/romagna.jpg",desc:"Romagna is a historical region of northern Italy between the Apennines, the Adriatic and the lower Po, now the eastern part of Emilia-Romagna. Its towns, among them Ravenna, Rimini, Forlì, Faenza and Cesena, were held by local lords under papal overlordship until Cesare Borgia and Julius II…"},
  {slug:"golden-temple",name:"Golden Temple",type:"work",dates:"c. 1830",img:"/images/encyclopedia/golden-temple.jpg",desc:"The Golden Temple, or Harmandir Sahib, at Amritsar is the holiest shrine of Sikhism. Under the patronage of Ranjit Singh of the Sikh Empire it was covered in gold leaf, a project completed around 1830, and it remains the defining monument of Sikh civilisation."},
  {slug:"bosphorus",name:"Bosphorus",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bosphorus.jpg",desc:"The Bosphorus is a narrow strait in northwestern Turkey connecting the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara and separating Europe from Asia. Constantinople stood on its European shore, controlling traffic between the two seas. This strategic position underlay the foundation and long survival of the…"},
  {slug:"keira-dynasty",name:"Keira dynasty",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/keira-dynasty.jpg",desc:"The Keira dynasty was the ruling dynasty of the Darfur Sultanate, founded in the seventeenth century by Sulayman Solong. It ruled until the defeat of Sultan Ibrahim in 1874. It was restored under Ali Dinar after 1898 until Darfur's incorporation into Anglo-Egyptian Sudan in 1916."},
  {slug:"nguyen-lords",name:"Nguyễn lords",type:"polity",dates:"1558–1777",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords.jpg",desc:"The Nguyễn lords were a family of hereditary rulers who governed southern Vietnam in the name of the Lê emperors from 1558. From their capital at Phú Xuân they resisted the Trịnh lords of the north in wars from 1627 to 1672, and extended Vietnamese settlement into the Mekong delta. Overthrown by…"},
  {slug:"azcapotzalco",name:"Azcapotzalco",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco.jpg",desc:"Azcapotzalco was the capital of the Tepanec state in the Valley of Mexico, which dominated the region in the late fourteenth and early fifteenth centuries. The Mexica of Tenochtitlan were initially its tributaries. In 1428 Tenochtitlan, Texcoco and Tlacopan defeated Azcapotzalco and formed the…"},
  {slug:"european-economic-area",name:"European Economic Area",type:"institution",dates:"1994",img:"/images/encyclopedia/european-economic-area.jpg",desc:"The European Economic Area is an agreement that came into force in 1994, extending the European Union's single market to member states of the European Free Trade Association. Iceland, Norway and, from 1995, Liechtenstein take part, adopting most EU single-market law without full membership. It…"},
  {slug:"adil-shahi-dynasty",name:"Adil Shahi dynasty",type:"group",dates:"1490–1686",img:"/images/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty.jpg",desc:"The Adil Shahi dynasty ruled the Bijapur Sultanate in the western Deccan from 1490, when Yusuf Adil Shah broke away from the Bahmani Sultanate, until the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb took Bijapur in 1686. Its sultans built the Gol Gumbaz and other monuments. Many Maratha and Lingayat families, among…"},
  {slug:"rhind-mathematical-papyrus",name:"Rhind Mathematical Papyrus",type:"work",dates:"c. 1550 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus.jpg",desc:"The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus is the largest surviving mathematical text from ancient Egypt, copied by the scribe Ahmose in the 33rd year of the Hyksos king Apepi from an original of the Twelfth Dynasty. It contains over eighty worked problems on fractions, arithmetic, areas, volumes and the…"}
